---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Three = `SEQUENTIAL   operations performed in order, one at a time
             takes as long as THE SUM OF ALL ITS STEPS

PARALLEL     the program broken into smaller sequential
             operations, SOME performed simultaneously
             one computer, multiple processors

DISTRIBUTED  multiple DEVICES used to run a program`;

const Formula = `A PARALLEL SOLUTION TAKES AS LONG AS

  its sequential tasks
    PLUS
  the longest of its parallel tasks

That is the exam's formula. It assumes
every parallel task gets its own processor.`;

const TwoProcessors = `BUT WHAT IF THERE ARE ONLY TWO PROCESSORS?

  Three tasks: 30s, 40s, 50s.  Two processors.

  The formula says: longest parallel task = 50.

  Try it:
     P1: 50            P2: 30 + 40 = 70   ->  70
     P1: 50 + 30 = 80  P2: 40             ->  80
     P1: 50 + 40 = 90  P2: 30             ->  90

  The best you can do is 70, not 50.`;

const Packing = `WHEN THERE ARE FEWER PROCESSORS THAN TASKS

  1  hand the tasks out among the processors
  2  add up each processor's total
  3  the answer is the BIGGEST of those totals
  4  try other arrangements -- you want the
     smallest possible biggest total

The formula is the special case where every
task gets a processor to itself.`;

const Speedup = `SPEEDUP

           time taken sequentially
  ------------------------------------
            time taken in parallel

  120 seconds sequential, 70 in parallel

           120 / 70 = 1.71`;

const Limit = `WHY MORE PROCESSORS STOP HELPING

  "the efficiency of the solution is still limited
   by the sequential portion. This means that at
   some point, adding parallel portions will no
   longer meaningfully increase efficiency."

If 50 seconds of the job cannot be split,
then 50 seconds is your floor. Forever.`;

const Checker = `let tasks = [20, 30, 40, 50];
let processors = 2;

function sequentialTime(list)
{
  let total = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < list.length; i++)
  {
    total = total + list[i];
  }

  return total;
}

// Hand the tasks out in the order you decide,
// then see what the slowest processor totals.
function makespan(list, assignment, howMany)
{
  let loads = [];

  for (let p = 0; p < howMany; p++)
  {
    loads.push(0);
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < list.length; i++)
  {
    let p = assignment[i];
    loads[p] = loads[p] + list[i];
  }

  let worst = 0;

  for (let p = 0; p < loads.length; p++)
  {
    if (loads[p] > worst)
    {
      worst = loads[p];
    }
  }

  console.log("loads: " + loads + "   finishes in " + worst);
  return worst;
}

let seq = sequentialTime(tasks);
console.log("sequential: " + seq);

// tasks 0 and 3 on processor 0, tasks 1 and 2 on processor 1
let par = makespan(tasks, [0, 1, 1, 0], processors);

console.log("speedup: " + (seq / par).toFixed(2));
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.10: Sequential, Parallel, and Distributed</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>Arithmetic, Not Vocabulary</h2>

          <p>
            <b>This session has real calculations in it</b>, and they are the kind the
            exam asks you to do. Three definitions, one formula, and one situation
            where the formula does not apply.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Do these on paper.</b> There is a program at the
            end you may use to check yourself, but the exam gives you a table and a
            pencil.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.10</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Sequential, Parallel, and Distributed</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Three Computational Models</p>
              <p>The Formula, and Its Assumption</p>
              <p>Speedup, and Why It Stops</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three Models</h2>
              <Code code={Three} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Definitions" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b>Parallel: one computer, several processors. Distributed: several devices.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Distinction Most Likely to Be Asked</h2>
              <p><b>Parallel</b> &mdash; multiple processors, one machine, shared memory.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Distributed</b> &mdash; multiple machines.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Distributed solves problems that <b>would not fit</b> on one computer &mdash; too slow, or too big to store.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Sequential Is Easy</h2>
              <p><b>The sum of all its steps.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">4 + 1 + 6 + 2 + 3 = 16 seconds. That is the whole rule.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Parallel Has a Formula</h2>
              <Code code={Formula} frame="code" lang="txt" title="CSN-2.A.6" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>And an Assumption</h2>
              <Code code={TwoProcessors} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Count the Processors" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">70, not 50.</b> The formula assumed three processors and you have two.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>So Read the Question</h2>
              <p><b>How many processors?</b> If the question says, that number matters.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Enough for every task &rarr; use the formula.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Fewer than tasks &rarr; <b>you have to hand the work out.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Handing the Work Out</h2>
              <Code code={Packing} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Four Steps" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">You are looking for the smallest possible biggest total.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Speedup</h2>
              <Code code={Speedup} frame="code" lang="txt" title="One Division" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Sequential divided by parallel. <b>Not the other way round.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Sanity Check</h2>
              <p>Speedup should be <b>greater than 1</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">If you get 0.58, you divided the wrong way round.</p>
              <p class="fragment">If you get 47 with two processors, something is wrong.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Parallel Is Worth It</h2>
              <p>Real things happen at the same time in different places.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Weather forecasting. Flight simulators. Crash modeling.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And parallel solutions <b>scale better</b> than sequential ones.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>But It Runs Out</h2>
              <Code code={Limit} frame="code" lang="txt" title="CSN-2.B.5" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Is a Familiar Shape</h2>
              <p>Adding processors stops helping once the sequential part dominates.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Adding data does not fix bias. Session 44.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>More of the thing you have is not always the answer.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Defining sequential, parallel and distributed computing</li>
                <li>Calculating the time taken by a sequential solution</li>
                <li>Calculating a parallel solution's time, with and without a processor limit</li>
                <li>Calculating speedup</li>
                <li>Explaining why adding parallelism eventually stops helping</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Sequential computing</dt>
                  <dd>Operations performed in order, one at a time. Takes as long as the sum of all its steps.</dd>
                  <dt>Parallel computing</dt>
                  <dd>The program broken into smaller sequential operations, some performed simultaneously.</dd>
                  <dt>Distributed computing</dt>
                  <dd>Multiple devices used to run a program.</dd>
                  <dt>Speedup</dt>
                  <dd>Sequential run time divided by parallel run time.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <p><b>Tasks 1 to 4 on paper.</b> Task 5 is optional and it is a checker, not a shortcut.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Sort the Three</h3>

          <p>For each, say <b>sequential</b>, <b>parallel</b>, or <b>distributed</b>.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>One processor works through a list of 400 names, one at a time</li>
            <li>Eight processors in one machine each handle 50 of those names</li>
            <li>Four hundred volunteers' home computers each check one name</li>
            <li>A weather model too large to fit in any single computer's memory</li>
            <li>Your Create Task program, running in a browser</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Then:</b> which of the three models could handle a problem the other
            two cannot, and what kind of problem is it?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Three Tables</h3>

          <p><b>Assume there are as many processors as tasks.</b> The tasks are independent.</p>

          <table class="table table-sm align-middle mt-3">
            <thead><tr><th>Set</th><th>Task times (seconds)</th><th>Sequential</th><th>Parallel</th><th>Speedup</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>30, 40, 50</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>20, 30, 40, 50</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>10, 20, 90</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p><b>Give speedup to two decimal places.</b></p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Now Count the Processors</h3>

          <p><b>Same three sets. Only TWO processors.</b> Each processor runs one task at a time.</p>

          <table class="table table-sm align-middle mt-3">
            <thead><tr><th>Set</th><th>Task times</th><th>Best split</th><th>Parallel time</th><th>Speedup</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>30, 40, 50</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>20, 30, 40, 50</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>10, 20, 90</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <ul>
            <li><b>Show the split you chose</b>, not just the answer.</li>
            <li><b>Which set is unchanged from Task 2</b>, and why?</li>
            <li>For set B, <b>now try three processors.</b> What happens, and why is that number familiar?</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Set B with two processors and then three is the
            most important pair of calculations on this page.</b>
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: A Sequential Portion</h3>

          <p>
            A job has a <b>50-second part that cannot be split</b>, then three
            independent tasks of 50s, 40s and 40s.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>How long does the whole job take <b>sequentially</b>?</li>
            <li>With <b>unlimited</b> processors for the parallel part?</li>
            <li>With <b>two</b> processors for the parallel part?</li>
            <li>With <b>a hundred</b> processors?</li>
            <li><b>What is the floor?</b> Explain what number the job can never go below, no matter how many processors you buy.</li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Task 5 (optional): Check Yourself</h5>

          <ul>
            <li>Run this and confirm it agrees with your Task 3 answer for set B.</li>
            <li>Then change the <code>assignment</code> list and find a <b>worse</b> split.</li>
            <li>Can you find one that ties with your best?</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Checker} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="A Checker" class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>This is a checker, not a method.</b> The exam gives you a table and a
            pencil, so the pencil version is the one that matters.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>How long does a sequential solution take?</li>
            <li>State the parallel formula, <b>and the assumption it makes</b>.</li>
            <li>Write the speedup formula. Which number goes on top?</li>
            <li>What is the difference between parallel and distributed computing?</li>
            <li>Why does adding processors eventually stop helping?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>In Task 3, set B took 70 seconds on two processors and 50 on three. Explain why the third processor helped and a fourth would not.</li>
            <li>Speedup for two processors is never more than 2. Explain why, and say what would have to be true for it to be exactly 2.</li>
            <li>Session 44 said collecting more data does not remove bias. Session 69 says adding processors eventually stops helping. Describe what those two statements have in common.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>All four tables completed with your splits shown, plus your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
